+# RFF70N06 operating-point investigation
+
+Status, September 10, 2026: **unresolved supported-scope convergence blocker**.
+The previously ignored `tests/regression/rff70n06_op.cir` is now represented by
+the required `RFF70N06.OriginalCorpusOperatingPoint` CTest. This adds a visible
+failure to the suite; it does not establish a new numerical regression.
+
+Checkpoint 13 also runs the unchanged fixture against a separately built
+ngspice 47. That reference aborts transient-OP fallback with a timestep-too-small
+error in the body diode; both frozen corpus variants become `BOTH_FAIL`.
+This does not resolve neospice's failure against ngspice 42 or remove the test.
+See [reference-version evidence](ngspice47-reference.md).
+
+Checkpoint 15 establishes an additional limitation of the version-42 result:
+its Newton driver discards a device convergence failure during this fixture's
+transient OP. The code path is corrected in 47. The original comparison remains
+required and failing, but the version-42 success alone is not evidence of a
+device-converged operating point. See the verified trace below; neospice's
+device-convergence checks have not been disabled to reproduce that acceptance.
+
+## Frozen input and reference behavior
+
+The input is `uncategorized/spice_complete/harprmos.lib::RFF70N06_HA` from
+KiCad-Spice-Library revision `a8688952bcaab19f567bc4db237b60bde03ef310`.
+[CMake acquisition](../tests/reference_models.cmake) pins the download URL and
+SHA-256 and verifies an offline override against the same hash. The
+[fixture template](../tests/circuits/rff70n06_op.cir.in) retains the original
+three 100 kOhm ground terminations and subcircuit pin order. No vendor model
+parameter or connection is changed. See [building](building.md) for acquisition
+and the remaining redistribution review.
+
+The reference uses the corpus's original `ngbehavior=psa`, required for this
+PSpice model's VSWITCH elements. A plain ngspice invocation without that mode
+fails model parsing and is not an equivalent reference run. The dedicated CTest
+process keeps the compatibility setting separate from other tests.
+
+ngspice 42 fails its direct, gmin and source-stepping attempts, then succeeds
+through transient operating-point fallback. Required external voltages from
+the shared-library reference are:
+
+| Signal | Reference voltage (V) |
+|---|---:|
+| `v(net_1)` | -33050.80998921323 |
+| `v(net_2)` | -33451.358017625695 |
+| `v(net_3)` | -33497.991777664516 |
+
+The test requires successful status, all three finite signals, and the original
+corpus allowance `abs(neo-ng) <= 1e-3*abs(ng) + 1e-6 V`. neospice currently
+fails before value comparison: 101 iterations and reported residual
+836.87533483480479. That field is the implementation's reported diagnostic,
+not an independently measured KCL residual; `newton.cpp` currently derives it
+from the loaded RHS magnitude.
+
+The model contains a floating internal network: reference nodes around
+`x1.17`–`x1.22` reach about 3.333e11 V, and `5O` is a distinct node from `50`.
+These observations motivate conditioning and fallback investigation; they do
+not justify changing the model, excluding the original fixture, increasing
+tolerances or declaring ngspice wrong.
+
+## Verified fallback correction
+
+ngspice `src/spicelib/analysis/optran.c` probes second-order truncation after a
+successful first-order step. It uses that probe's proposed interval for the
+next step, including when the order returns to one. neospice selected the order
+from that probe but retained the first-order interval. It now carries forward
+the second-order interval.
+
+`Convergence.OpTransientUsesSecondOrderStepProposal` exercises the driver with
+a controlled device step constraint. The third interval must be 0.95 ns when
+retaining order one and 1.5 ns when promoting to order two; the old driver used
+2 ns in both cases. Both checks failed before the correction and now pass in
+Release and ASan/UBSan. This is a driver-contract regression, not a physical
+reference-circuit comparison. **RFF70N06 still fails after the correction.**
+
+## Diagnostic reference build
+
+The local ngspice source was clean at revision
+`902a62d2f442a1d8322ae4fcad35c143c7a14561`. A separate CLI build enables existing
+trace output without modifying that source or the fixture:
+
+```sh
+mkdir -p /tmp/neospice-joss-ng42-trace
+cd /tmp/neospice-joss-ng42-trace
+/home/subhagato/Codes/ngspice/configure \
+  --prefix=/tmp/neospice-joss-ng42-trace/install --enable-xspice \
+  --disable-debug --without-x --with-readline=no CFLAGS='-O1 -g -DSTEPDEBUG'
+make -j8
+make install
+install/bin/ngspice -D ngbehavior=psa -b \
+  /tmp/neospice-paper-audit-build/tests/generated/rff70n06_op.cir
+```
+
+Use the generated fixture path for the chosen neospice build directory. This
+diagnostic build also succeeds through transient OP; it supplements the system
+ngspice 42 baseline rather than replacing it. The compressed
+[trace](evidence/joss/2026-09-10-rff70n06-ng42-trace.log.gz) and
+[checkpoint 9 record](evidence/joss/2026-09-10-validation-09.json) preserve its
+output, commands and hashes. This trace is not performance evidence.
+
+## Checkpoint 15: startup history and reference convergence flag
+
+The OPtran driver now initializes older timestep intervals with `dt_max`, as
+ngspice initializes `CKTdeltaOld`, and seeds `state2` from the entry state before
+the first predictor. The main transient driver receives the same older-interval
+correction. Before correction, its second candidate exposed a zero older
+interval. The startup-history regressions failed on these conditions and pass
+after the fixes. **RFF70N06 still fails**; these corrections alone do not resolve
+its trajectory difference.
+
+Full-precision diagnostics narrow the remaining investigation. At the first
+accepted OPtran point, compared external-node voltages differ by approximately
+1.5e-8 V between neospice and the ngspice 42 diagnostic build. At the next
+candidate, neospice's weak MOS instance proposes approximately 4.00007 ps,
+while its inductors propose approximately 4.8 ps. The reference trace selects
+4.79976 ps from an inductor, with no tighter MOS proposal. The diagnostic
+neospice objects add output only; removing their extra lines reproduces the
+uninstrumented verbose trace byte for byte. Reference `%g` formatting was
+expanded to 17 digits by a diagnostic printf wrapper; all 160 retained time
+diagnostics agree with the earlier trace at its original six-digit precision.
+
+The source and debugger establish why device histories may differ even at
+nearly identical terminal voltages:
+
+1. In 42, `MOS1convTest` sets `CKTnoncon=1` on failure and returns `OK`.
+2. `CKTconvTest` sees the flag, stops checking further devices, and also returns
+   `OK`.
+3. `NIconvTest` returns that code without checking the flag.
+4. `NIiter` assigns the returned zero to `CKTnoncon`, losing the device failure.
+
+On the unchanged RFF fixture, gdb observes the first three transient MOS checks
+entering with flag zero, returning `OK` with flag one, and `NIconvTest` returning
+zero while the flag is still one. The subsequent assignment loses it, and the
+reference finishes transient OP successfully. A separate state inspection at
+the first truncation call shows the weak MOS's limited gate-source history at
+about -4.3442e6 V while its terminal difference is about -3.3330e11 V.
+
+Version 47's `src/maths/ni/niconv.c` explicitly tests `CKTnoncon` after
+`CKTconvTest` and returns one when a device fails. Load-time nonconvergence
+flags are distinct and are honored by both versions. This finding must not be
+generalized into a claim that ngspice 42 has no device convergence checks.
+
+`Convergence.RejectsDeviceFailureWithSettledTerminalVoltages` protects
+neospice's behavior with an unsettled-device negative control and a settled
+positive control. It retains device failure even when terminal voltages stop
+changing. The failing RFF numerical comparison remains visible; it has not been
+replaced by this control or reclassified as a pass.
+
+Evidence is retained in the [checkpoint 15 record](evidence/joss/2026-09-10-validation-15.json)
+and [diagnostic archive](evidence/joss/2026-09-10-startup-checkpoint15.tar.gz).
+Next: establish the release reference policy with this failure-flag distinction
+explicit, continue supported numerical-failure investigation against 47, and
+retain the original 42 comparison as historical evidence. This proves a
+reference acceptance defect, not universal correctness of neospice or the
+absence of other RFF defects.
