+# Transient readiness investigation
+
+This page retains the transient investigations from checkpoints 14–17,
+September 10, 2026. Supported-scope failures remain. See
+[progress](joss-progress.md) for complete results and [validation methods](validation-methods.md)
+for the comparison formula. No acceptance limits were increased.
+
+## Checkpoint 17: breakpoint scheduling follows ngspice 47
+
+The source comparison shows that `MOS3load` is byte-identical between the two
+reference releases, while `dctran.c` changes the approach to a breakpoint.
+Version 47 halves the remaining interval when the proposed step is within a
+factor of 1.9 of the corner, unless it already reaches the final time within
+100 floating-point ULPs. A step crossing the corner is still shortened to land
+on it. The stop time is itself a breakpoint.
+
+Neospice now follows this scheduling rule. Its former ten-percent snap could
+enlarge a timestep to reach a nearby corner; that rule is removed. Three unit
+regressions failed before the change and now pass: two balanced approach steps
+at several time scales, no enlargement, and final-time handling including the
+100/101-ULP boundary. The existing circuits, options, assertions and tolerances
+are unchanged.
+
+| Transient comparison | Maximum normalized error against 47 | Existing tolerance |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| JFET2 switching, worst required signal | 1.69407e-9 | 0.1 |
+| MOS3 pulse, drain voltage | 0.00760383079854 | 0.05 |
+| VBIC switching, collector voltage | 6.74565e-10 | 0.27 |
+
+All three pass, as does the previously failing constant-transmission-line
+time-grid comparison. The complete 47 suite now passes 1,191 of 1,195 tests.
+The full historical 42 suite passes 1,190 of 1,195: it now also fails the MOS3,
+VBIC and constant-line grid comparisons, alongside RFF70N06 and JFET2. These
+version-dependent failures are retained; results are never selected per fixture
+from whichever reference passes. Future release reference/CI policy must account
+for these incompatible schedules and other version-dependent syntax.
+
+The expanded sanitizer run against 42 passes 323 of 345 selected tests. The 22
+failures include two numerical-only comparison failures, 15 reference-library leaks
+and five reference-library buffer overflows; the new MOS3 numerical failure adds
+to checkpoint 15's list. The same instrumented engine and three transistor test
+drivers were additionally linked against the unchanged 47 library: all 14 tests
+pass with leak detection enabled and no sanitizer reports. The reference library
+itself is not instrumented, so this does not certify all reference memory paths.
+The rebuilt wheel passes all 335 Python/tooling tests.
+
+See [checkpoint 17 evidence](evidence/joss/2026-09-10-validation-17.json).
+The completed checkpoint 15 corpus runs predate both the MOS3 convergence change
+and this scheduling change. Final candidate corpus evaluation remains open.
+
+## Checkpoint 16: MOS3 convergence callback
+
+MOS3 previously exposed only the `MOS3load` nonconvergence flag. The adapter now
+also implements the drain and bulk current checks in ngspice's `MOS3convTest`,
+using the newly solved voltages, the limited load-state voltages, and the active
+simulation's current tolerances. The load flag remains required. The reference's
+distinct drain (`>=`) and bulk (`>`) rejection boundaries are preserved.
+
+A regression settles a transistor, perturbs its gate voltage without reloading
+the device, and requires rejection while the unperturbed solution remains
+accepted. Both polarities and channel directions are exercised, with a preceding
+stateful device. All four changed-gate probes were incorrectly accepted before
+the fix. The six MOS3 tests pass against 42 and under ASan/UBSan with leak detection.
+
+This correction **does not resolve** the MOS3 transient difference against 47:
+the maximum normalized drain error remains 0.051831943570428876 at the unchanged
+0.05 tolerance. No existing full-suite failure disappears or is waived.
+
+The callback audit also establishes a distinction for the next investigation:
+ngspice 47 registers no `DEVconvTest` for JFET2, while `VBICconvTest` reads
+`CKTrhsOld` rather than MOS3's `CKTrhs`. Adding the MOS3 solution-vector check
+to either adapter without examining those semantics would not reproduce the
+reference. VBIC buffer semantics and the remaining transient differences are
+still open. Source snapshots and verification are retained in
+[checkpoint 16](evidence/joss/2026-09-10-validation-16.json).
+
+## Corrections and evidence
+
+The old restart path reduced `dt` at a source corner, then doubled that reduced
+value in the next-step growth proposal. The restart bound now applies to the
+final proposal. In the LTRA RC diagnostic, ngspice advances from 1 ns to 1.01 ns,
+while the old driver advanced to 1.02 ns. The corrected fixture returns identical
+values on all 214 shared timestamps against 42. The existing whole-waveform
+assertion remains required.
+
+Like ngspice `dctran.c`, the driver now retains the speculative second-order
+truncation proposal even if the method remains first order. MOS3's maximum
+normalized drain error against 42 is 2.90e-15. The first transient load also
+initializes charge history that is unavailable in the preceding DC solution;
+copying that initialized history into deeper states prevents invalid early
+truncation estimates. VBIC's collector error against 42 is now 3.64e-8 at the
+unchanged 0.27 tolerance. Device truncation starts on the second candidate;
+the separate global voltage estimator retains its own history requirement.
+
+The old absolute termination checks could return a converged result before
+the requested stop time. The loop now runs to the requested stop and explicitly
+fails if floating-point time cannot advance. Analytic constant-source tests
+cover stop times 1e-19, 1e-17 and 1e-15 seconds. A smallest-subnormal-step test
+requires failure in both throwing and `no_throw` modes. Controlled device probes
+test the second candidate and order-two proposal; an ideal pulse tests restart
+spacing. These driver contracts supplement physical circuit comparisons.
+
+## Remaining JFET2 discrepancy
+
+The unmodified fixture uses `PULSE(-2 0 0 1n 1n 5u 10u)`. Its second falling edge
+begins at 15.001 us and ends at 15.002 us. Standalone shared-library traces retain
+295 points from ngspice 42 and 297 from 47. Near this edge:
+
+| Reference | Sample time (us) | Gate voltage (V) |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| 42 | 15.001 | 0 |
+| 42 | 15.002222111361192 | -2 |
+| 47 | 15.001 | 0 |
+| 47 | 15.0011 | -0.2 |
+| 47 | 15.0013 | -0.6 |
+| 47 | 15.00165 | -1.3 |
+| 47 | 15.002 | -2 |
+
+In `src/spicelib/devices/vsrc/vsrcacct.c`, 42 selects pulse corners using strict
+comparisons of the period-relative time. Version 47 selects with
+`atime = time + ckt->CKTminBreak`, while computing the wait from the original
+time. Its accompanying comment explains that a request too close to the current
+time is ignored, so selection must advance to the next corner. The retained
+source diff and trace establish a reference sampling difference at this edge.
+They do not establish that neospice agrees everywhere or justify accepting the
+failed comparison.
+
+The current JFET2 comparison still fails against both references. Against 42,
+the maximum normalized gate-current error is 6.34765 near 15.0011 us. Against
+47, it is 0.508656 near 1.17504 ns, at the unchanged tolerance 0.1. MOS3 and
+VBIC also still fail against 47. Further source/history/timestep investigation
+is required; neither version is substituted selectively to improve pass counts.
+
+## Verification limits
+
+The full suites retain successful and failed comparison details in JUnit with
+the output limit increased to 1 MiB per test. Earlier intermediate logs remain
+diagnostics and do not certify the final source. The expanded sanitizer suite
+retains reference-library failures; see the checkpoint record for individual
+names, outputs and source hashes. Historical corpus results predate this driver
+change, and final candidate evaluation remains outstanding.
